{{also|equivócate}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|æquivocate||archaic}}

===Etymology===
From Late {{inh|en|enm|equivocaten}}, from {{der|en|ML.|aequivocātus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|aequivocō||I am called by the same name}}, from {{der|en|LL.|aequivocus||ambiguous, equivocal}}. Compare {{cog|fr|équivoquer}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ɪˈkwɪvəˌkeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-equivocate.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To speak using [[double meaning]]; to speak ambiguously, unclearly or doubtfully, with intent to [[deceive]].
#: {{syn|en|prevaricate|evade|shuffle|quibble|adumbrate}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|act=II|scene=iii|page=137|column=1|passage=''Knock''. Knock, knock. Who's there in th'other Deuils Name? Faith here's an Equiuocator, that could ſweare in both the Scales againſt eyther Scale, who committed Treaſon enough for Gods ſake, yet could not '''equiuocate''' to Heauen: oh come in, Equiuocator.}}
#* '''1687''', {{w|Edward Stillingfleet}}, ''The Unreasonableness of Separation: Or, An Impartial Account of the History, Nature and Pleas of the Present Separation from the Communion of the Church of England''
#*: {{quote|en|All that Garnet had to say for him was that he supposed he meant to '''equivocate'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To render [[equivocal]] or ambiguous.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=George Buck|title=Reign of Richard the Third|year=1647|passage=He '''equivocated''' his vow by a mental reservation}}

